ildren  the doctor was keen to show us  the youngest victim that they'd had  and she was in an incubator in intensive  care  and she had this huge bandage on her  head we're still  checking the ct scan of the brain and to  see how extensively is  bleeding that's tragic  that is tragic yeah she was four days  old  a little baby called sophie she's got  lots of lacerations  to her head that was the big worry  whether she'd been affected  neurologically or not  overwhelmingly you know you've got this  feeling of helplessness you know this  poor little  bundle in this you know incubator is  days old and  just have to go through you know this  explosion and the doctors  weren't sure whether or not they would  be able to operate  seeing as a tiny small  newborn baby so badly hurt  we were coming out of the children's  intensive care and i saw  two young people sitting outside  our newborn baby she's like four days  old  in the intensive care i think i think  we've just seen her  [Music]  i was breastfeeding my baby and the  explosion  explored everything in the house  look at me i tried to protect her  but i couldn't do more than this  they wanted to tell us everything about  what what had happened what's your first  baby  how are you coping we're doing  our best we  it was horrific so so difficult to hear  someone's  testimony like that and it did make me  think  just how many other mothers are in this  situation  and how many mothers are now and fathers  are grieving this morning  today because of what what's happened  because they've been through so many  terrorist atrocities  because they were also in the middle of  a massive  economic meltdown as well as  a coronavirus pandemic this explosion  came at a time when  the country the city was already on its  knees  very soon after the explosion it emerged  that there had been 2 750  tons of one of the most combustible  chemicals  sitting in hangar 12 for about  six years look how this huge  warehouse if we cross now kev 