 the likelihood of us even getting in and  now doing a live report from the scene  of this  explosion was improbable kev was  desperately trying to get the thing to  work  we were in the middle of nowhere in a  sense because everything had been  destroyed around  us so it wasn't working and suddenly  kev turns around and says right i've got  a live signal we can do this  this is ground zero and the people in  blue that you can see  are members of the russian search and  rescue team  this was the first time that most people  were up so close and  right at the epicentre of the explosion  [Music]  the diggers are working here they really  don't expect to find  anyone there alive anymore and because  of the  level of destruction it very much is a  recovery  operation here at least  not only were they trying to find  survivors  they were also trying to find conclusive  evidence of what had happened  to people and at the beginning they just  kept on finding body parts  every family we spoke to who had loved  ones  either killed or missing at that time  their anger  was very clearly directed at the state  to many lebanese it seemed like a  massive catastrophe just waiting to  happen  and it had been sitting there waiting to  happen  for many many years  anger was building by the hour they  realized that  none of this needed to have happened  beirut just exploded in anger  i think in those protests people did not  go down to the streets  looking for culprits they were looking  for accountability  and now they wanted someone to face up  to that reality  and in their eyes it wasn't just the  government  that was responsible it was the  political establishment in lebanon  i think the explosion is when people hit  rock bottom it's when they realize that  actually  the likelihood of things getting better  any time soon  and the dangers that they were facing on  a daily level were so great that it  wasn't worth it  it wasn't worth the sacrifice but i  think that they feel totally hopeless  now  and that is heartbreaking  what really touched me about this uh  story i mean i've been to lebanon before  but it was really different working on a  disaster of this  magnitude with a colleague  who's actually lebanese  been at the seat of the tragedy but also  was  absolutely determined to carry on  working  it was surprising how well he was coping  i think it was just a determination to  tell the story and get it out there  lebanon as a country has got so many  scars now  and so many open wounds  i hope what happened that day may  eventually lead to  massive changes sadly many people  believe that it's going to take the  country  years and years to get over it  next time on hot spots the guy in charge  is like out of a tarantino movie  i'm in chihuahua in mexico where a story  about people smuggling  become something else entirely here was  a story  about one of our most precious natural  resources water  and my team are in houthi-controlled  north yemen  where they've now got coronavirus on top  of the horrors of civil war  80 of the population of yemen cannot  survive without  help until then you can find us on  twitter  at alex crawford sky and at ramsey sky  see you next time  [Music]  bring me  [Music]
 

